    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures). Here's what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: a new owner's `membershipLevel` is capped at one above the highest `membershipLevel` among their existing household members (same last name + postcode); with no existing household member, no cap applies.\n\nSince `membershipLevel` is a *derived* value recomputed by the mapper on every fetch, the cap has to persist so a re-fetch returns the capped value. I stored the ceiling on the owner and applied it in the mapper.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a nullable `membershipLevelCap` field (column `membership_level_cap`) with getter/setter. `null` means \"no cap.\"\n\n2. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 `membershipLevel(owner)` now computes the raw level from points as before, then clamps it down to `membershipLevelCap` when that cap is set.\n\n3. **`rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 on create, after resolving `householdMembers`:\n   - empty household \u2192 cap `null` (no cap),\n   - otherwise cap = `max(membershipLevel(member)) + 1` over existing members (each member's own cap is respected, since it goes through the mapper).\n\n4. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java` (household gate)** \u2014 the previous behavior rejected any same-household owner with **409** unless the caller set `sharesHousehold`. That gate fired *before* the cap could ever apply, so the new test's second household member (distinct telephone/email, no flag) was rejected. Since the cap is exactly what makes adding household members safe \u2014 and an exact byte-for-byte identity duplicate is still caught by the earlier identity-key check \u2014 I removed that rejection so a distinct additional household member is admitted and capped. The `sharesHousehold` flag now only suppresses the possible-duplicate flagging.\n\n5. **`db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql`** \u2014 added the `membership_level_cap` column so the JPA mapping resolves (tests run on hsqldb; the other three kept consistent).\n\nOne thing worth flagging: point 4 relaxes an existing household-duplicate rejection. The provided spec text only describes the cap, but the new test creates a second same-household owner *without* `sharesHousehold` and expects `2xx` \u2014 which is impossible while that 409 gate stands. I made the narrowest change that satisfies the test (exact-identity duplicates are still rejected); let me know if you'd rather the gate stay and the flag be required instead.",
